From: HeDo <ldileo@digipa.it>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:48:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] The scale of the quadrics is computed with multiplications,
 not pow

With ScaleIndependent the costs of the collapses are multiplied by 1e8 / diag^6, which was
computed with pow. The last bit of pow is not the same in every mathematical library, and the
factor scales the cost of every collapse: the order of two collapses of almost the same cost, and
the comparisons with QuadricEpsilon, could differ between platforms. The power is now the product
of two cubes, whose bits IEEE arithmetic fixes.
---
 .../local_optimization/tri_edge_collapse_quadric.h         | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization/tri_edge_collapse_quadric.h b/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization/tri_edge_collapse_quadric.h
index bf5d0d0..4e76be2 100644
--- a/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization/tri_edge_collapse_quadric.h
+++ b/vcg/complex/algorithms/local_optimization/tri_edge_collapse_quadric.h
@@ -601,7 +601,12 @@ public:
     {
       vcg::tri::UpdateBounding<TriMeshType>::Box(m);
       //Make all quadric independent from mesh size
-      pp->ScaleFactor = 1e8*pow(1.0/m.bbox.Diag(),6); // scaling factor
+      // scaling factor: 1e8 / diag^6, by multiplications, which give the same bits everywhere
+      // (pow does not: the last bit of its result is not the same in every mathematical library,
+      // and the factor scales the cost of every collapse)
+      const double inverse = 1.0/m.bbox.Diag();
+      const double cube = inverse*inverse*inverse;
+      pp->ScaleFactor = 1e8*(cube*cube);
     }
 
     if(pp->QualityWeight) // we map quality range into a squared 01 and than this into the 1..QualityWeightFactor range
